@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
' '   description:  The same checkout interaction as a communication diagram, contrasted with the sequence-diagram equivalent in a note.
'   usecase:      Contrast communication (structure + numbers) vs sequence (lifelines + time); explain the tradeoff.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       e-commerce
' ============================================================================
title Example Communication Variant - Communication vs Sequence

' COMMUNICATION vs SEQUENCE: two ways to draw the SAME interaction.
'
' COMMUNICATION DIAGRAM
'   - Objects are nodes; associations are LINKS between them.
'   - Messages ride the links with SEQUENCE NUMBERS (1, 1.1, 2, ...).
'   - Emphasizes STRUCTURE: who is connected to whom.
'   - Reading order comes from the numbers, not spatial position.
'
' SEQUENCE DIAGRAM
'   - Objects are LIFELINES (vertical dashed lines descending from each object).
'   - Messages are horizontal arrows between lifelines, ordered TOP-TO-BOTTOM.
'   - Emphasizes TIME: the vertical axis is a strict timeline.
'   - Activation bars show when each object is active.
'
' TRADEOFF:
'   - Use COMMUNICATION when the network of links is the interesting part —
'     e.g. you want to see the static collaboration topology at a glance.
'   - Use SEQUENCE when the ORDERING and timing of messages is the interesting
'     part — e.g. you need to see which call happens before which, or you
'     want activation bars to reason about concurrency and blocking.
'   - Communication diagrams become hard to read once message counts get
'     high (numbers sprawl); sequence diagrams handle high counts better
'     but bury the link topology.
'
' BELOW: the COMMUNICATION version is rendered. PlantUML cannot cleanly
' render two diagram TYPES in a single @startuml frame, so the equivalent
' SEQUENCE diagram is described in detail in the closing note.

actor "Customer" as Customer
participant "Cart" as Cart
participant "Checkout" as Checkout
participant "Payment" as Payment

Customer --> Cart : 1: addItem(item)
Cart --> Checkout : 2: requestCheckout()
Checkout --> Payment : 3: authorize(amount)
Payment --> Checkout : 3.1: returnAuthCode()
Checkout --> Cart : 4: clearCart()
Checkout --> Customer : 5: confirmOrder()

note over Customer, Payment
  <<equivalent sequence diagram>>
  Same interaction drawn as a sequence diagram would show:

    Customer     Cart      Checkout    Payment
       |          |            |          |
       |--1: addItem(item)---->|          |
       |          |            |          |
       |          |--2: requestCheckout()>|
       |          |            |          |
       |          |            |--3: authorize(amount)-->|
       |          |            |          |
       |          |            |<--3.1: returnAuthCode()--|
       |          |            |          |
       |          |<--4: clearCart()------|          |
       |          |            |          |
       |<--5: confirmOrder()---|          |
       |          |            |          |

  Key differences a reader should notice:
    - The vertical axis is TIME (top = earliest, bottom = latest).
    - Lifelines (vertical dashed lines) replace the link network.
    - Activation bars (narrow rectangles on a lifeline) show WHEN each
      object is actively executing — a notion communication diagrams lack.
    - The structural LINKS (Cart-Checkout, Checkout-Payment) are implicit
      in the arrows but not drawn as first-class associations.
end note
@enduml
